swehouse.c: the Placidus pole height follows the cusp

Placidus and the Gauquelin sectors used AscDash() alone, which holds the pole
height fixed. It is a function of the cusp, so the total derivative needs the
implicit function theorem. plac_speed() supplies it with both partials in closed
form.

Two further changes ride on the same twelve lines and cannot be separated from
it, because all three edit the same statements.

The first is the |tant| < VERY_SMALL branch inside the six iteration loops.
That branch sets a speed and breaks, and the plac_speed() call after the loop
then overwrote it with a pole height belonging to an earlier iterate. It is
guarded now.

The second is the same test's other site, before the loops, where the first
estimate is seeded. It reported hsp->armc_speed, which drops the obliquity
factor; it now reports AscDash() at the pole height that belongs to the cusp.
That site is live rather than defensive: instrumented against the
unpatched library it fires 53,240 times in 5,181,120 Placidus and Gauquelin
calls, about one in a hundred, and the value it substitutes differs from the
old one by 22 per cent on average and by up to a factor of 8.2. It has no
demonstration of its own here because reaching it takes a cusp landing on the
ecliptic equator, which no swetest command line lands on directly.

This step also carries pullen_sr_sectors() and pullen_sr_slopes(), which step 08
uses. They sit in the same block as the plac_* helpers and splitting the block
leaves a piece git apply will not place.

Part of an eight-step series. Applied in order, the eight produce a file
byte-identical to the single patch "swehouse.c: compute house cusp speeds
analytically"; take either the series or that patch, not both. Every step
compiles. Steps 01 to 06 also apply to a clean tree on their own, so any one of
them can be taken without the rest; 07 needs 01 to 05, not 06; 08 needs all seven to link. Do not
drop 02 or 04 from the middle: nothing else needs them, so the result still
builds clean and is quietly wrong.

